WEBVTT

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.080
There’s English bread,
German bread, and French bread,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.960
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.500
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.870
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:17.930
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.180
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.510
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.100
Translated by: Anime-Empire
Yakitate!! Japan
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.430
Yakitate!! Japan
Translated by: Anime-Empire

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:39.440
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.
Yakitate!! Japan
Translated by: Anime-Empire

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.070
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:53.900
Under the night sky, we, who are whistling...

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:01.660
without words, are just tracing constellations with our fingers.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:09.130
The dreams of the cold-sensitive... Your cold hands...

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:18.510
The warming magic is to believe in one path.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:27.270
The single star found on the other side of the comet cloud...

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.530
whether it be a shining or a faint star...

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.490
is the light of only you.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:38.270
On the other side of the cloud in your heart,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.080
is a sign post that remains unseen.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:50.800
Now, opening these hands, what will you believe in today?

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.260
As the first big step towards completing
<i>Japan</i>, the bread that tastes better than rice,

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.260
Azuma Kazuma challenged the new employee examination

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.640
at the charisma of the bakery world:
Pantasia’s main store.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.850
He made Indian naan-style bread for the first exam

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.650
and overwhelmed Kuroyanagi, the examiner.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.310
By noon tomorrow, make a croissant.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.110
The new employee shall be decided by this test.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.440
Who is this Kurowa-san?

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.620
Who in the world is Kurowa-san?

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.050
Burnt Black!!

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.880
Is This the Final Kurowa-san!?

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:30.970
Okay, okay.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.430
This is Azuma.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.050
Kazuma?

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.550
Huh? Kurowa-san?

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.070
Yeah, I want to find out
who Kurowa-san is.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:43.370
Do you know, Inaho nee-chan?

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.100
Beats me.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.310
I wonder if Jii-chan would know?

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.310
Hey, Jii-chan, do you know Kurowa-san?

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.160
Dam.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.660
Isn’t that Kurobe dam, Jii-chan?

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.820
Only the “kuro” part matches...

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:57.790
So...?

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.950
I thought it was a joke, but that guy really
doesn’t know what a croissant is.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.340
This is great.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.670
This knocks one out.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.180
Only two rivals remain.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.890
Azusagawa Tsukino and Suwabara Kai.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.040
Suwabara?

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:13.720
Wait a minute...

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.220
I’ve heard that name before.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.150
This isn’t good!

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:20.930
That guy is...

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:22.980
I’m at a loss.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.400
Neither Jii-chan nor Nee-chan know.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.570
Who in the world is Kurowa-san?

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:29.920
Is something the matter?

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.790
That bread is the icon of France.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:38.660
Oh, really?

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.130
You really didn’t know?

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.370
Yeah. I’ve only ever made <i>Japan</i>.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.460
<i>Japan</i>?

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.590
I don’t really know what you mean,

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.930
but shall I teach you how to make a croissant?

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.470
Are you for real?

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:53.220
Yes.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.500
Hey, come to think of it...

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.100
It was the same during the first exam...

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.140
Why are you so nice to me?

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.300
Why did you stick up for me that time?

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.050
Despite how I may seem,

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.570
I’m a good judge of character.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.560
Especially when it comes to bread artisans.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.060
Judge...?

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.820
In that case, shall we head over to the workshop...

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.200
to learn about croissants.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:16.400
Hold it!

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:18.290
Kawachi.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.630
Umm... He got kidnapped.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.300
It’s just as I thought.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.170
Those hands are definitely Solar Hands.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.680
You’re going to teach me, Kawachi?

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.440
I’m thankful, but it seemed she was going to teach me.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.520
What’s the use of just learning how to make it?

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.190
What we need to make...

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:52.100
is the ultimate croissant that can beat even Suwabara.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:54.990
Who’s this?

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.610
Open your eyes and have a look.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:58.560
It’s Suwabara.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.960
He was the highest-ranking young contender
in a first-class bread-making skill competition,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.250
and was the winner of the contest
for best new bread artisan.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.420
On top of that, the theme of the contest...

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.080
was the croissant.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:13.700
Incredible!

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.550
Got it? I’ll put it simply.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.590
In other words... you and I, alone, have
no chance of winning against him.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.090
But even so, if we work together,
we have a chance at beating Suwabara.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.110
Work with me, and let’s create an ultimate croissant.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:30.300
I don’t want to.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.060
Y-You don’t want to?

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.120
If we don’t make it by ourselves, there’s no point, right?

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.270
It’s also kind of cowardly.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.120
I’ll get Tsukino to teach me how to make it, so it’s fine.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.890
K-Kawachi?!

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.760
I’m begging you, it’s my life’s request.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.350
If I cannot remain after this exam, I...

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.430
My little brother and sister...

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.360
will become homeless!

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.860
Nii-chan, we’re out of rice.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.280
Nii-chan, I’m hungry.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.380
H-Homeless?

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:09.160
My parents died in a fire last year.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.910
I’ve had to look after
my siblings ever since.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:16.170
So I’ve got to earn some money...

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.080
Nii-chan, I’m cold.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:20.000
Nii-chan, I’m hungry.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.900
There’s nothing we can do
but starve to death.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:28.430
Uh?

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.610
Oops, maybe I went a bit too far.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.380
Is this guy a moron?

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:46.130
If it’s discovered that we made it together,
we could be disqualified.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.170
U-Um... let’s make the croissant together, okay?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.850
Um, regards to my partner.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.580
The same in return,
let’s do our best, comrade.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.390
This is it... These hands...

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.810
With these Solar Hands,
we can surely win.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.820
I’m the one who will win.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.860
Got it? This is the most important part.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:21.120
Okay.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.370
I’ll just confirm...

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.660
Suwabara has no deductions, and is still on 10 points.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.640
Next is me on 8 points, and you’re on 3 points.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.010
8 points and 3 points!

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:33.720
W-What?

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.090
If we add our points together,
we have 11!

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.010
We’re 1 point up on Suwabara!

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.730
Umm...

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.300
That’s right! That’s exactly it.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.390
Well noticed. You’re a genius.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.730
That’s what it is, right?

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.060
That’s it, after all!

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:48.520
Is this guy a moron?

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.480
I’m a real genius! That’s really it.
Is this guy a moron?

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.480
Hm? Come to think of it, that girl...
I’m a real genius! That’s really it.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.400
Hm? Come to think of it, that girl...

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.320
How many points did
Azusagawa Tsukino have?

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:57.120
Oh well.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:01.450
So, croissant means “crescent.”

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.620
In other words, its shape is from the crescent moon.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.130
That changed over time, and became croissant.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:09.380
I don’t care about that.
Teach me how to make it.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:10.750
Geez...

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.600
Show some interest if you’re aspiring to be a baker.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:14.630
Hurry up.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.350
Fine, I got it.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.010
I’ve put in the ingredients you told me to.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:22.590
In that case, knead the dough as I say.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.210
Once the moisture has spread through,
add some butter and mix it in.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:28.860
Like this?

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.600
It’s done.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:33.320
Ok.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.230
Next, take the dough from the bowl
and knead it until it’s settled.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:41.690
He’s pretty good for his first time.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.570
And the dough has already started to ferment.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:46.820
It must be those hands.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:51.660
It seems Kawachi-san has noticed those hands, too.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.420
I can’t wait for the result.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.350
Next,

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:02.980
we mold the butter like this, and flatten it to a square.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.230
Do we put that butter between the dough
and flatten it, by any chance?

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:09.050
Yeah.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.150
That’s similar...

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:13.780
to <i>Japan</i> #43.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:16.190
He’s still saying that.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.030
Well, that’s nice.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:22.690
Wrap the butter in the dough, fold into two, and flatten.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.780
Fold the flattened dough into three, and flatten.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.950
Fold this into three, twice more, and you get 54 layers.

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:33.050
This is the dough used to make the standard croissant.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:34.750
That’s too few.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:35.300
What?

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:36.710
Listen,

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.170
if you make the dough thin with too many layers,

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.810
those layers, which are the lifeblood
of a croissant, become crushed.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:48.050
On top of that, we used 60 grams of
sugar for every 350 grams of flour,

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.640
and croissant dough with a high sugar
content burns easily if it’s too thin.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:55.570
At most, we can fold it once more
into two, for 108 layers.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.940
That’s the limit.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.980
Like I said, that’s too few.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.240
What are you saying, you amateur?

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.570
It should be 324 layers.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.210
I can do that with my <i>Japan</i>.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.710
T-Three hundred and twenty?!

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:12.790
Are you a moron? If you bake something
like that, it’ll be burnt to a crisp.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.200
What’s wrong with that,
does that taste bad?

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:16.200
Of course it does.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:18.720
What?

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.790
The important part is what’s inside...

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:26.380
What did you say?

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:27.480
You can do that?

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.300
Leave it to me.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.480
If it’s <i>Japan</i> #43’s dough, we can do it.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:35.480
We take this 108 layer dough, and
fold it a further three times.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:37.770
Is this really okay?

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:39.810
Is something the matter?

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:40.940
You see,

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:43.650
a 324 layer croissant...

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.650
You seem to be having fun together.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.710
I beg you, please keep this a secret.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.870
Otherwise, my siblings will be homeless...

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:58.470
Don’t worry.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.760
Secret or otherwise, it doesn’t matter.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:04.680
What’s that supposed to mean?

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.970
Kawachi, the dough is ready.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.020
What do I do now?

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:13.400
Hello again.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.220
Come with me.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:16.980
Yes?

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.390
Kawachi, I’ll leave the rest to you.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.280
H-Hey!

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:30.990
Oh well.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.370
We folded the limit of 108 layers,
by an additional three times.

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:39.120
If I’ve got this 324 layer dough
called <i>Japan</i> or whatever...

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:43.340
Now, I use a kitchen knife to cut this
dough into isosceles triangles.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:47.350
This one triangle becomes a croissant.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.840
A completed croissant, ready for baking.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:54.400
Guess I’ll make some for him, too.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:01.900
Oh, what a surprise.
They can come apart.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.410
One layer becomes this thin.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:09.570
What’s with the way this tears?

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.780
It’s like paper being cut

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.420
by a well-made Japanese sword,
in the hands of a master.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:19.200
Protein, known as the framework of bread...

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.960
The gluten matrix needs to be in straight
lines for it to tear like this.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.930
If I were to name this,
I’d call it Solar Dough.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:29.510
The warmth of Solar Hands, which
is above that of body temperature,

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.380
allowed the yeast germ fermentation
to be perfectly uniform.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.300
I can win...

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.270
against Suwabara!

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:40.100
That Azuma has only 3 points left.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.280
I have 8 points.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:45.780
If we both present identically delicious croissants,

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:50.040
with my 5 point lead, I’ll surely end up the winner.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.860
I’ll have the last laugh!

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:06.000
You will now present the task of your final exam.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:10.010
Show me an ultimate croissant, worthy of Pantasia.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.950
There are many things I’d like to ask,
but first of all...

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:34.410
Kawachi, what is that lump of charcoal?

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.540
It’s... a croissant.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.670
It seems a total amateur has found its way in here.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:42.500
Croissant

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:45.630
“Kurowassan” it may be...
but it’s “kuro-wassan,” isn’t it?
Croissant

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:50.420
To bake something without burning it
is the most basic of all basic skills.

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:54.220
I already knew if would turn out like this.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:55.550
But, that guy...

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.980
Hey, where’d you go with that girl?

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.980
Never mind that, how did the croissants turn out?

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:06.900
I just tried baking them at the
standard temperature setting.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:08.860
As I predicted, this is what happened.

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:10.730
This is no good!

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:12.830
Yeah, and that’s what I said!

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:16.620
You haven’t baked it long enough.
You have to burn it more.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:18.360
More?!

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:19.330
Burn it?!

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:21.580
Didn’t I tell you?

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.620
What’s inside is most important..

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:24.920
After that, you use this.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:25.920
Right?

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.930
It’s not “Right?” at all, seriously...

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.710
Can I really do it with this, <i>Japan</i>-tarou?

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:34.710
Number 34.

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.930
It seems number 35 is not here?

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:41.440
Come to think of it, <i>Japa...</i> I mean,
Azuma doesn’t seem to be around.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.640
And I don’t see that girl here, either.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.650
Surely they aren’t still off somewhere together...?

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:48.740
About Azuma-san...

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:52.490
He has pulled out of the new employee examination.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:54.660
What was that?!

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:58.610
Y-Young lady...

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.170
And... that jii-san is...?

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:04.950
Azusagawa Sadanao!

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.250
Owner of Pantasia Group.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.960
I’ve seen him in books...

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:08.970
Why is Azusagawa Tsukino, one of
the applicants, with the owner...?
I’ve seen him in books...

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:13.170
Why is Azusagawa Tsukino, one of
the applicants, with the owner...?

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:14.050
Azusagawa!

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:15.050
Azusagawa!
Azusagawa!

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:15.710
Azusagawa!

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:16.470
No way!
Azusagawa!

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:16.480
Azusagawa!

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:20.890
She’s the owner’s, Azusagawa’s
grandchild, and is still young...

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.680
but in charge of Pantasia’s southern Tokyo store.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:25.480
What did you say?!

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:27.480
So that’s the trick you’ve been playing.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.900
She’s been amongst the applicants, just on a whim,

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.530
but she has nothing to do with the
new employee examination.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:39.360
Secret or otherwise, it doesn’t matter.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.040
So that’s what she meant.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.990
Revealing my dishonesty means nothing,

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:46.240
if she’s on the recruiting side after all.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:48.700
It’s over.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:53.510
That moron Azuma must have pulled
out because he caught on to this.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.050
So I’m the only one who gets exposed?

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.300
To think I’d have to choose between
Suwabara and a guy like this...

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:03.220
This is a disappointing development.
I still have to make a decision.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.220
I will now begin the tasting for the final exam!

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:10.190
I really don’t want to eat such charcoal...

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:13.400
It can’t be helped...
The tasting was a promise.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:14.480
I’ll eat it.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:15.730
Don’t touch it yet!

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:17.190
What did you say?

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.530
It may be over for me,

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.320
but at the very least I will complete this creation!

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:29.170
What?!

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:30.330
No way.

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.960
I-It really...

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:36.080
It really came out!

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.630
The moon came out!

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.020
The crescent moon that floats in the night sky!

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:43.380
What’s going on?

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:46.560
From within the charcoal, a beautifully
baked croissant has...!

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:50.470
The taste...

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:56.520
This is Houston.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:58.610
Apollo 12, report your status.

00:16:58.610 --> 00:16:59.870
This is number 12.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.740
We are now stepping onto
the moon surface.

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.080
Inside my mouth...

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:12.960
there’s a light, spinning sensation.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.920
To the man who made it...

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.170
it’s just a single, small croissant...

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:20.670
but it is one giant leap
for the bread industry.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:23.970
It has a feeling of toughness.

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:26.550
Just how many layers does this have?

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:29.260
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7...

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:31.390
No!! This is more than 300 layers!

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:33.390
Dough made with a lot of sugar
burns easily.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.650
But to compensate for that,

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:37.660
you made the dough with this many layers?!

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.070
Behold! This is it!

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:44.900
1mm thickness, a croissant with 324 layers minus 10.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:49.530
The outer-most 10 layers or so are not to be eaten.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.590
They are for trapping the taste within.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:54.630
Plus 6 points!

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:56.870
P-Plus 6 points?

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:59.460
Added to my 8 points, that’s 14.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.010
I’m 4 points ahead of Suwabara!

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.350
Next, Suwabara’s croissant...

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:06.220
Wait a minute.

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:09.350
Let me also taste that.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:11.140
Okay...

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:25.080
He’s knocked out, impossible.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:27.110
I stand defeated.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:27.910
It’s good...

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:30.080
Better than mine.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:33.490
That isn’t for you to judge.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:34.590
It’s my job.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:35.670
Shut up.

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:37.120
That’s enough.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.960
We already have our answer.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.180
Number 34, Kawachi-kun.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:46.930
I have decided to recruit you to
Pantasia’s main Tokyo store.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:49.680
Oh...

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:51.510
Congratulations.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:53.520
Congratulations on your recruitment.

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:55.890
U-Um...

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:58.400
Azusagawa Tsukino-san, come with me.

00:18:58.770 --> 00:18:59.230
Yes?

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:06.280
The one who baked it at the very end was me.

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:07.000
Yes.

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.540
You understand that, right?

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.870
Despite how I may seem, I’m a wicked person.

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.500
If you don’t watch what you say, I won’t
be responsible for what, happens.

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:16.250
Wow, scary.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.410
Azuma may have noticed

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:21.880
that you were on Pantasia’s side,
and on to our cheating,

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:24.340
so he pulled out of this examination...

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:27.770
But I’m not like that. I won’t let
a chance like this get away.

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.760
You seem to be a little mistaken.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:31.520
What?!

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:34.260
I think the reason Azuma-san pulled out,

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.810
was because he thought of your siblings.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:39.530
That time...

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:42.970
I’ll drop this new employee examination.

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:47.740
You know, I’ve never lied to other people before,

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.040
so in this situation, I just don’t know
what expression to put on...

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.410
If this lie was to show on my face,

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:58.510
the fact that Kawachi and I made the croissant together would be found out, right?

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.920
Then I’d be making trouble for Kawachi.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.420
Kawachi’s siblings
would end up homeless.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.000
Azuma-san...

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:07.760
That’s about it.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:12.770
I, a person who has only ever made
<i>Japan</i>, wouldn’t be of much use to a bakery.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:19.680
Pantasia needs someone knowledgeable who’s able to make anything, like Kawachi.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:20.640
Don’t you think so?

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:24.310
So he’s backing out...

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.690
That’s what he said.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.450
There are limits to being kind.

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.910
This is why country people
are troublesome.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.460
Well, those types should
just be left alone.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:40.170
You just can’t deal with country people.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.300
Even though I say this...

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:46.590
I’m a country boy, too...

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:58.430
What should I do now?

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:02.690
If I return home like this, I’d probably
get beaten by Nee-chan...

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:07.410
Who did that, dammit?!

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.410
Kawachi... Why?

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:14.110
Why did you have to hit me?

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:15.870
I mean, why are you here?

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:17.820
Thanks to you, I failed.

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:20.290
Did they find out?

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:23.620
As if I could beat Suwabara with a croissant like that.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:26.430
That examiner occhan saw that it was burnt black!

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:29.500
Because of that, they hired Suwabara.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:31.920
What’re you saying?
Didn’t you use the spoon?

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.930
You don’t even know
how to use a spoon?

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:38.890
A thing called a spoon
is used to scoop something.

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:40.820
You’re a spoon.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:43.480
Um, I don’t understand.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:44.440
What does that mean?

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.190
Don’t worry about it, let’s go.

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:47.950
Go...? Where?

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:49.820
Hello Work.
Note: “Hello Work” is the name of Japan’s government employment agency.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:50.700
What bakery is that?
Note: “Hello Work” is the name of Japan’s government employment agency.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.680
It’s not a bakery.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:54.950
I’m not interested unless it’s a bakery!

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:01.200
So that croissant was something they made together...

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.450
And Kawachi-kun pulled out, too.

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:06.470
But it’s difficult to let that
talent go to waste.

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:10.130
We should have recruited those two.

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.470
Oh, Ojii-sama,
please don’t say such things.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:15.300
I don’t approve.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:19.090
I had my eyes on them from the beginning, after all.

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:23.230
Those two...

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:28.270
I want them...
for my southern Tokyo store.

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:46.700
If I think of you, and even utter the clichéd phrase “I love you,”

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:55.710
it makes such a nice sound, and you continue talking.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:06.720
The hopeless tears your bold self sometimes shows me form flag of truce.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:11.910
If nothing is said, then all the more,

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:18.040
I just can’t look away. Things like sadness and pain...

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:20.600
Well, that happens a lot in life, after all.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:24.740
When you casually say, “Do your best,” it gives me strength.

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:29.510
Whenever your small back trembles, I will embrace you right away.

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:33.350
Even if the scenery outside my window changes...

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.050
Stay with me as you are, bad sleeping habits and all.

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:43.240
If you sulk, and utter

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:52.050
Even the clichéd “I hate you,” it comes across as the opposite.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:24:01.390
I will love you, no matter what. It’s just another Sunday to me.

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:08.350
Make a delicious bread!

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.040
Afro hair!

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.310
Note: “Ossan” is just another form of “occhan,” used in reference to older men.
Another weird ossan has appeared.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:15.690
Next episode, Yakitate!! Japan.

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:16.000
Hihiin!!
Note: Both say “umai pan” which means “delicious bread,” but the second version replaces “u” with the kanji for “horse,” as a pun.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:16.850
Hihiin!!

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:18.560
Make a Delicious Bread!

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:20.050
Be sure to see it!

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:22.800
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:23.260
Straight
Crescent

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:24.820
At the center of production in France,
Straight
Crescent

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:26.240
they use margarine for the crescent-shaped croissant,
Straight
Crescent

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:28.120
Straight
they use margarine for the crescent-shaped croissant,

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:29.610
and butter for the straight croissant type.
Straight

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.820
and butter for the straight croissant type.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:33.450
I see!
